{
 "id": "1966-gould-mental",
 "name": "Mental maps: ranking the 48 states by where you would like to live",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the ranking task in Peter Gould's \"On Mental Maps\" (Michigan Inter-University Community of Mathematical Geographers, Discussion Paper No. 9, 1966), the study that started the mapping of residential desirability. Students in beginning geography courses at the state universities of California (Berkeley), Minnesota, Pennsylvania and Alabama (about 25–50 per university) ranked the 48 contiguous states of the United States in order of preference as places to live, imagining complete freedom of location; where a state was hard to place they were asked to compare it with the others one by one (\"If I had to choose, which would I prefer?\"). Participants here build the same ranking by tapping states on a tile map or a list of names, can move states up and down, and see their own mental map at the end. Analysis as in Gould: rank correlations between all pairs of respondents in a group, principal components of that matrix, and the scores of the states on the first component mapped as a \"perception surface\"; compare groups from different home regions. The page stores the full order, the rank of each state, the number of adjustments and the time used. Method taken from the open reprint of the discussion papers on archive.org (archive.org/details/micmog). No images are needed. Differences from the original study: (1) the ranking is made on screen with a simplified tile map of the states instead of a printed list on paper; (2) the instruction is shortened from the paper's description of the task, since the original answer sheet is not printed; (3) home state, current state, age and gender questions are added by us so that groups can be compared; (4) researchers who study another country can replace the list of states in the page. The template is taken down if the rights holders object.",
